        "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/s/c0e974/{size}.png",
        "created_at": "2015-12-07T18:19:10.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>Aside from \"order of operations,\" there's another way to look at it.&nbsp; Redirected damage is always considered to come from the source to the end target and not be affected by effects that apply only to the target(s) not taking damagebecause of the redirection.&nbsp; This is most easily visible with Nightmist's amulet or Fixer's Mantis style.&nbsp;</p><p>For examplre, Baron Blade's Backlash Field.&nbsp; The Baron strikes Nightmist for 5 damage; she uses her amulet to redirect to the Baron.&nbsp; Legacy's +damage bonuses do not apply to the damage jujsyt because it \"bounced off\" Nightmist; the game considers Baron Blade to be attacker AND target.&nbsp; The Backlash field will attack <em>Blade</em> in retaliation.</p><p>The \"middleman/woman/robot/alien\" is not considered to be part of the damage sequence.&nbsp; It's always worked trhis way with \"normal\" damage <em>reduction, </em>too, like Haka's Ta Moko.&nbsp; If damage going to Haka is redirected to Legacy, it doesn't get to \"keep\" the -1 reduction from Ta Moko and apply it to the damage hitting Legacy.</p><p>If that principle is applied, when Lead from the Front redirects the damage from Fanatic, cards that affect damage to Fanatic (such as Undaunted) would not apply because Plague Rat is now directly damaging Legacy.</p>",
        "post_number": 8,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T04:30:05.393Z",
